{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-sonnet-5-5","id":"msg_011Cg6kzF4JE2LCrqkgX9QKT","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Here's a practical regex:\n\n```regex\nhttps?://(?:[a-zA-Z0-9-]+\\.)+[a-zA-Z]{2,}(?::\\d{1,5})?(?:/[^\\s]*)?\n```\n\n**How it works**\n- `https?://` matches `http://` or `https://`.\n- `(?:[a-zA-Z0-9-]+\\.)+[a-zA-Z]{2,}` matches the domain: one or more labels followed by a dot, then a TLD of 2+ letters.\n- `(?::\\d{1,5})?` matches an optional port.\n- `(?:/[^\\s]*)?` matches an optional path, query string and fragment, up to the next whitespace.\n\n**Matches:** `http://example.com`, `https://sub.example.co.uk:8080/path?q=1#frag`\n\n**Doesn't match:** `ftp://example.com`, `http://localhost` (no TLD)\n\n**Variations**\n- To also allow `localhost` and IP addresses, use a looser host: `https?://[^\\s/$.?#][^\\s]*`.\n- If URLs appear in prose, trailing punctuation like `.` or `)` gets captured. To avoid that, end the path with a negative lookbehind: `https?://[^\\s]+(?<![.,;:!?)\\]])`.\n- To match the whole string, wrap it in `^...$`.\n\nExample in JavaScript:\n\n```js\nconst urlRegex = /https?:\\/\\/(?:[a-zA-Z0-9-]+\\.)+[a-zA-Z]{2,}(?::\\d{1,5})?(?:\\/[^\\s]*)?/g;\n\"Visit https://example.com/a?b=1 now\".match(urlRegex); // [\"https://example.com/a?b=1\"]\n```"}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":6756,"cache_read_input_tokens":11227,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":6756},"output_tokens":2,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global","speed":"standard"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"559cdda7-525d-4be2-a457-ab47cea6b4d5","uuid":"5d9aba19-b6b4-4e92-ab22-7327b02b36a5","timestamp":"2026-10-16T23:09:45.460Z","request_id":"req_011Cg6kzEpArB4g3rxSNTu68"}
